xnu 0153: load the touch firmware, so the part can actually report

The part has no firmware of its own: the vendor driver writes an ISP stage and
five sections (223744 bytes) into it on every boot. Until it does, the IC info
block answers (length 193, config 0x46) but its misc fields read as zeros — so
touch_data_addr comes back as 0x1 and the "reports" the 0152 thread reads from
0x10308 are noise.

Read the package from a blob area in front of the log ring
(mondrian_ufs.c: mondrian_reserved_read; 256 KiB reserved, written once from
recovery with dd, no file system involved), then follow
goodix_brl_fwupdate.c for Berlin D: reset, hold the CPU (0x0002 = 01 00, read
0x2000 until three reads agree), misctl at 0xD804 = 0x20700000, watchdog off at
0xD040, ISP to 0x23800, eight 0x55 bytes at 0x10000, reset and check that
patch_pid[3..5] is "ISP", then each section in 4 KB packets (data plus a 32-bit
checksum to 0x12410, then a 0xBB command with its own checksum to 0x12400, ack
and status 0xEE).

The blob also carries the sensor config (1488 bytes, type 4 at 0x3e000), which
the vendor flashes before the five firmware sections; tools/mk-touch-blob.py
repacks it as table entry 1, so the kernel's table order matches. The update
resets use brl_reset's 2 ms low pulse, not brl_power_on's 15 ms one.

The report thread starts only after a successful load, and the IC info is read
after it, when misc carries the real touch_data_addr. The loader also prints
patch_vid, sensor_id and the config ID at 0x10076, so one boot log says whether
the part is running the firmware or still the ROM.

